// keybindings.windows.json — a sample Windows/Linux-flavored tecode user
// keybindings file (Issue #115, replacing the removed "windows"
// keybindings.preset). Copy this to ~/.config/tecode/keybindings.json (or
// %APPDATA%\tecode\keybindings.json on Windows), or merge these entries
// into your own file — same plain JSONC `{ key, command, when? }` shape
// documented in samples/keybindings.json.
//
// Deliberately small — and, as shipped, deliberately a NO-OP. Read this
// before assuming the entries below change anything.
//
// This codebase's defaults are already VS-Code-on-Windows/Linux-shaped
// throughout (ctrl-based shortcuts everywhere), so there was little left
// for a "windows" scheme to change. The removed bundled preset claimed
// one difference: editor-core binds move/duplicate-line to alt+meta+up /
// alt+meta+down / shift+alt+meta+down, which LOOKS like a macOS idiom
// (meta = Cmd), so the preset added alt+up / alt+down / shift+alt+down as
// the "Windows/Linux-native equivalents".
//
// That premise was wrong, and those three bindings never fired. The meta
// token there is not a Cmd key at all: Alt+Arrow decodes through
// @opentui/core's modifier-bitmask branch, which sets `option` AND `meta`
// from the SAME Alt bit, so keyEventToStroke always emits BOTH tokens for
// an Alt-held arrow on EVERY platform — a bare alt+up stroke is never
// produced for anyone, on Windows or anywhere else, and an entry bound to
// it is unreachable (packages/builtin/editor-core/manifest.ts's "Alt+Arrow"
// TSDoc documents the decoding, verified against the CSI \x1b[1;{n}A form,
// the double-ESC \x1b\x1b[A form, and both Kitty and legacy parsing).
//
// Corrected to the strokes that actually arrive, the three entries become
// identical to the defaults they were supposed to override. So this file
// adds nothing on its own, and is kept only as a correctly-shaped starting
// point to EDIT: the header format, the `when` clause, and three real
// command ids to copy. If you want genuinely different Windows/Linux keys,
// change the `key` values below (and check `keybindings.showResolved` from
// the command palette to confirm what actually took effect).
[
  // NOTE: these three restate editor-core's CURRENT defaults exactly, so
  // copying this file as-is changes nothing. That is not an oversight —
  // see the header above for why, and treat these as a starting point to
  // EDIT rather than as bindings that add anything on their own.
  //
  // They must carry BOTH "alt" and "meta" to match at all. Alt+Arrow
  // decodes through @opentui/core's modifier-bitmask branch, whose own
  // logic sets `option` AND `meta` from the SAME Alt bit
  // (`key.option = key.option || !!(modifier & 2); key.meta = key.meta ||
  // !!(modifier & 2);`), so `keyEventToStroke` always emits both tokens
  // together for an Alt-held arrow — never "alt" alone
  // (`packages/builtin/editor-core/manifest.ts`'s "Alt+Arrow" TSDoc
  // documents this as verified against the CSI `\x1b[1;{n}A` form, the
  // double-ESC `\x1b\x1b[A` form, and both Kitty and legacy parsing).
  // A plain "alt+up" entry can therefore never fire.
  { "key": "alt+meta+up", "command": "editor.action.moveLinesUp", "when": "editorTextFocus" },
  { "key": "alt+meta+down", "command": "editor.action.moveLinesDown", "when": "editorTextFocus" },
  { "key": "shift+alt+meta+down", "command": "editor.action.duplicateLine", "when": "editorTextFocus" }
]
